# Rust-first MetaMap rebuild and functional parity

Date: 2026-10-11. Project name: **Patamaps**, chosen by the owner. R0–R11 are implemented for the declared draft profiles on ARM64 macOS and Linux. **The intended full functional parity with MetaMap is unfinished. Section 9 is the active P0–P9 remediation backlog and replacement acceptance gate.** Native routing, application topology, replay, checked laws/proofs, typed uncertainty, risk budgets, external approvals, protected activation, bounded inspection, workspaces and scientific integration are delivered. [Naming and verification](docs/acceptance/2026-10-11-patamaps.md) identifies the renamed candidate; [earlier acceptance](docs/acceptance/2026-10-11-final.md) remains historical evidence. Commit `d519705664f95d8a614f870868faf0b311eb6953` publishes the initial implementation and evidence. No tagged release or production activation has occurred.

**Recommendation: rebuild the compiler and protected activator in Rust, while retaining MetaMap's semantics, portable data, reference cases, and useful discovery adapters.** A line-by-line TypeScript translation would preserve implementation debt. Replacing every adapter and contract would discard working interoperability and increase migration risk. The useful boundary is a new Rust implementation of the existing correspondence compiler, introduced through a declared compatibility profile and a staged consumer migration.

Rust owns graph validation, scoped authority, admission, derivations, uncertainty, budgets, projection, replay, explanations, governance verification, and protected activation. Language-specific discovery can continue in TypeScript or Python and emit portable shards. Generated consumers execute ordinary native structures; they do not require the graph compiler at runtime.

This document makes the README's implementation sequence actionable. The README controls product scope; this plan controls implementation order and acceptance. Section 9 supersedes earlier scope restrictions wherever they omit public MetaMap functionality. MetaMap's pinned contracts and tests define reference obligations. New contract decisions must record differences; declaring a difference does not establish the owner's acceptance of a parity exception. Test expectations and historical results are preserved evidence, not permission to deploy or proof that the new implementation is correct.

